    expectedAnswer: "Use two pointers when two positions can move through a sequence while an invariant lets you discard candidates safely. Common forms are opposite-direction pointers on sorted data, same-direction read/write pointers, and fast/slow pointers on linked structures.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Identify the pattern signal.\n\nStep 1 — Sorted Two Sum.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction twoSumSorted(\n  nums: readonly number[],\n  target: number,\n): [number, number] | null {\n  let left = 0;\n  let right = nums.length - 1;\n\n  while (left < right) {\n    const sum = nums[left]! + nums[right]!;\n\n    if (sum === target) return [left, right];\n    if (sum < target) left++;\n    else right--;\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / Map alternative:\n```ts\nfunction twoSumWithMap(\n  nums: readonly number[],\n  target: number,\n): [number, number] | null {\n  const seen = new Map<number, number>();\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    const needed = target - nums[i]!;\n\n    if (seen.has(needed)) {\n      return [seen.get(needed)!, i];\n    }\n\n    seen.set(nums[i]!, i);\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n[1,2,4,7,11], target=9\n1+11 too large → move right\n1+7 too small → move left\n2+7 = 9 → found [1,3].\n\nComplexity:\nSorted two-pointer O(n) time, O(1) auxiliary space.\nMap version O(n) average, O(n) space.\n\nDecision rule:\nOnly move a pointer when the ordering or invariant proves the discarded region cannot contain the answer.\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nUse the pattern only when its invariant and assumptions actually hold.",
