    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Identify the pattern signal.\n\nStep 1 — Exact search.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction binarySearch(nums: readonly number[], target: number): number {\n  let left = 0;\n  let right = nums.length - 1;\n\n  while (left <= right) {\n    const mid = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);\n\n    if (nums[mid] === target) return mid;\n    if (nums[mid]! < target) left = mid + 1;\n    else right = mid - 1;\n  }\n\n  return -1;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction binarySearchUsingIndexOf(\n  nums: readonly number[],\n  target: number,\n): number {\n  return nums.indexOf(target);\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n[1,3,5,7,9], target=7\ncheck 5 → go right\ncheck 7 → found.\n\nStep 2 — Answer-space variant:\nFor problems like minimum capacity or minimum speed, binary-search a numeric answer and write `can(value)` to test feasibility.\n\nComplexity:\nExact binary search O(log n).\n`indexOf` is O(n).\n\nDecision rule:\nAsk: \"Is the predicate monotonic?\" If yes, binary search may work even when there is no literal sorted array.\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nUse the pattern only when its invariant and assumptions actually hold.",
