    expectedAnswer: "Use DFS when the problem asks to explore an entire connected component, recursively inspect a tree/graph, detect cycles, generate paths, or compute values bottom-up from descendants. Mark visited nodes to avoid repeated work.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Identify the pattern signal.\n\nStep 1 — Graph DFS.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction dfsOrder(\n  graph: ReadonlyMap<string, readonly string[]>,\n  start: string,\n): string[] {\n  const visited = new Set<string>();\n  const result: string[] = [];\n\n  function dfs(node: string): void {\n    if (visited.has(node)) return;\n\n    visited.add(node);\n    result.push(node);\n\n    for (const next of graph.get(node) ?? []) {\n      dfs(next);\n    }\n  }\n\n  dfs(start);\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / iterative stack:\n```ts\nfunction dfsOrderIterative(\n  graph: ReadonlyMap<string, readonly string[]>,\n  start: string,\n): string[] {\n  const visited = new Set<string>();\n  const stack = [start];\n  const result: string[] = [];\n\n  while (stack.length > 0) {\n    const node = stack.pop()!;\n    if (visited.has(node)) continue;\n\n    visited.add(node);\n    result.push(node);\n\n    const neighbors = graph.get(node) ?? [];\n    for (let i = neighbors.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n      stack.push(neighbors[i]!);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nA → B, C\nB → D\nDFS from A → A,B,D,C (depending on neighbor order).\n\nComplexity:\nO(V + E) time for an adjacency-list graph.\nO(V) visited/stack space in the worst case.\n\nDecision rule:\nDFS is natural when depth, recursion, components, or path exploration matters.\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nUse the pattern only when its invariant and assumptions actually hold.",
